The following code is used to output assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in a csharp method.

```cs

using System;

public class AssertStatementGenerator
{
    public static void GenerateAssertStatements(string code)
    {
        string[] lines = code.Split('\n');

        foreach (string line in lines)
        {
            if (line.Trim().StartsWith("@@@"))
            {
                string statement = line.Trim().Substring(3);
                Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({statement});");
            }
        }
    }
}

```

To use the `GenerateAssertStatements` method, provide the csharp code as a string argument. The method will split the code into individual lines and check if each line starts with "@@@". If it does, it extracts the statement and outputs the corresponding debug assert statement.

Note: The code assumes the presence of a `Debug` class with a `Assert` method. Make sure to import the necessary namespaces and update the class name if necessary.--------------------
Debug.Assert(index <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
</code>

The following code block will be produced as the assert statement corresponding to the comment "@@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array".--------------------
Debug.Assert(arrList.Count == Old_arrListCount && index <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
</code>
Given the csharp code:
```cs
    // @@@ index passed in is less than or equal to last index of x in new array 
```
The corresponding assert statement is:
```cs
Debug.Assert(index <= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
```
Now, If there are more assert statements, they would be outputted similarly.--------------------
Debug.Assert(index <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
